NeqSim is a Java library for thermodynamic fluid properties and process
simulation. The full licence text is at the URL above and is not reproduced
here.

### What is reused

**The component databank. `data/components/`** is generated from NeqSim's
`data/COMP.csv` and `data/INTER.csv` by `tools/gen_databank.py`, which converts the
columns to SI once and records the conversions. 348 substances - NeqSim's
`COMP.csv` filtered to the component types a model here reads - and 957 binary
interaction pairs.

Neither file is redistributed as it stands. **The 62 ion rows are carried, and a cubic
is refused over one.** They are included for the columns an electrolyte model reads -
`IONICCHARGE`, `DeshMatIonicDiameter`, `MOLARMASS` and `DIELECTRICPARAMETER1..5` - and
never for their critical constants, which are filler: one shared default set on 27 of
the 62 rows, a neutral parent's numbers on most of the rest (`MDEA+` carries MDEA's
unchanged), and no stated source for the remainder. So `mixture_of` refuses a cubic over
anything NeqSim files under `COMPTYPE = 'ion'` rather than computing with those numbers.
`COMP_EXT.csv` is not used at all.

**Algorithms.** `eos.critical_point`, `eos.stability_test` and the models built on
the same machinery. Each spec's notes record how its implementation differs from
NeqSim's and why.

### Why this file exists, and what the obligation is

NeqSim is licensed under Apache-2.0 and azoth is licensed under AGPL-3.0-or-later.
Apache-2.0 is **one-way compatible** with the GNU licences: Apache-2.0 code may be
incorporated into an AGPL-3.0 work, and the result may be distributed under
AGPL-3.0. The obligation Apache-2.0 imposes is **attribution**, not copyleft
reciprocity - a derivative work must carry the licence, retain the copyright and
attribution notices it received, and state the changes it made. That is what the
sections above and below are for.

Two things make this simpler than it might be. NeqSim ships **no per-file licence
headers** (0 of its 3,371 source files carry one) and **no NOTICE file of its own**,
so there is no downstream notice to reproduce verbatim. And every reuse here is of
an **algorithm**, which is not copyrightable in the first place - what is protected
is the expression, so what is owed is credit and a statement of the differences,
which is what this file gives.

### The data, and the one risk that remains

A compilation can carry its own copyright even where the individual facts in it do
not, so vendoring NeqSim's tables rests on the grant rather than on the facts:
`COMP.csv` is part of the work distributed under Apache-2.0, and §4 permits
redistributing it with attribution. That is the whole basis, and it is why this
paragraph is not the one that covered the Crane coefficients - those are not
licensed for redistribution at any price, and these are.

What Apache-2.0 does not establish is that every value inside the compilation was
cleanly sourced upstream. That is not inspectable from here, and the mitigation is
attribution rather than inspection: the source is named, so a reader who needs to
know where a number came from knows where to look.

### Changes

Recorded in each calculation's spec, in its own notes - which is where the design
decisions already live - rather than in a block kept for the purpose. What each
spec says is how its implementation differs from NeqSim's and why, because those
differences are the part a reader cannot recover from either side.

