Model Context Protocol - the specification's JSON Schema
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Project:   Model Context Protocol - https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol
  Version:   the 2026-07-28 revision at commit 271ecc9, and the 2025-11-25 revision at c4c367f
  Licence:   Apache-2.0. The project is transitioning from the MIT License, and "all new code and
             specification contributions to the project are licensed under Apache-2.0" - these two
             revisions are specification contributions, and the licence file states it directly.
  Vendored:  python/tests/validation/vendor/mcp/, unmodified
  Source:    schema-2026-07-28.json https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/271ecc9/schema/2026-07-28/schema.json
             schema-2025-11-25.json https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/c4c367f/schema/2025-11-25/schema.json
  Digest:    schema-2026-07-28.json sha256 ef70b61f99b6d2e5e3b46863822eab08dff6a45bedc7a08914e0e5b133f40203
             schema-2025-11-25.json sha256 268a5f82ba70fd7e4b6dc4aa1e64f116f74b4d0edcb69dc046829c79dd4e97e7

`azoth mcp` is a hand-rolled implementation of a published protocol, and the one thing such an
implementation cannot check about itself is whether it is *conformant*: it writes what its author
read. The specification publishes a JSON Schema per revision, whose `$defs` name the message types,
so `python/tests/validation/test_mcp_conformance.py` validates every message **both transports**
write - `azoth mcp` over stdio and `POST /mcp` on `azoth serve` - against the definition for the
revision that message belongs to. That is why it is vendored rather than fetched at test time: the
schema is the *pin*, and a check that fetched it would pass on a revision nobody has read.

**Two files, because the protocol has two lanes.** `2026-07-28` removed the `initialize` handshake
and has no `InitializeResult`; `2025-11-25` has no `server/discover`. The server answers both, so
each lane is held to its own schema - and the split is load-bearing rather than tidy: a
`CallToolResult` missing the current revision's required `resultType` fails one schema and passes
the other.

The vendor directory is under the *test* rather than beside the transport it validates. The
transport is Rust; the validator is Python, because `jsonschema` is already a dev dependency and a
Rust JSON-Schema implementation would be a new one for a test.

Nothing is changed: the two files are copied byte for byte from the URLs above. The digest is
recorded because neither file names its own revision - neither carries an `$id` - so the URL and
the digest above are the whole of what pins them. They were checked against upstream `main` on
2026-09-26, and they matched.

**Both halves of the pin are read back by `tools/check_mcp_schema.py`.** The digests are held to the
vendored bytes on every push, in CI's `spec-validate` job; the URLs are re-fetched by a scheduled
workflow, where a fetch that fails is reported as *undecided* rather than as a revision having
moved. Without the second half the sentence above ages: a new revision upstream would leave this
repository conformant to a specification that had moved on, with every gate green.
